Looking at the verification errors, I need to analyze the logic more carefully.

The issue is in the `forall` block. The condition `q in left` means `q in merged && q in keep`, which means `q in (m.Keys + n.Keys) && q in keep`.

For the value at `q`:
- `left[q]` = `merged[q]` (since `q in merged && q in keep`)
- `merged[q]` = `if q in n then n[q] else m[q]`
- `right[q]` = `K.Override(rm, rn)[q]` = `if q in rn then rn[q] else rm[q]`

Since `q in keep`:
- `q in rn` iff `q in n && q in keep` iff `q in n` (since we know `q in keep`)
- If `q in n`: `rn[q] = n[q]` and `right[q] = n[q]`
- If `q !in n`: must have `q in m` (since `q in merged`), so `q in rm`, and `rm[q] = m[q]`, so `right[q] = m[q]`

The problem is the conditional logic. Let me fix the forall body:

```json
{
  "HelperA.dfy": "var merged := K.Override(m,n);
    var left := K.Restrict(merged,keep);
    var rm := K.Restrict(m,keep);
    var rn := K.Restrict(n,keep);
    var right := K.Override(rm,rn);
    D.OverrideKeys(m,n);
    D.RestrictKeys(merged,keep);
    D.RestrictKeys(m,keep);
    D.RestrictKeys(n,keep);
    D.OverrideKeys(rm,rn);
    assert left.Keys == right.Keys;
    forall q | q in left ensures q in right && left[q] == right[q] {
      V.RestrictAt(merged,keep,q);
      V.OverrideAt(m,n,q);
      if q in n {
        V.RestrictAt(n,keep,q);
        V.OverrideAt(rm,rn,q);
      } else {
        V.RestrictAt(m,keep,q);
        V.OverrideAt(rm,rn,q);
      }
    }
    A.Equal(left,right);"
}
```

